00:03:02.345 --> 00:03:02.970
AUDIENCE: Waze.

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.720
FELIPE HOFFA: Waze,
kind of public data.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:07.070
But Google.

00:03:07.070 --> 00:03:09.800
I like that answer
because if you go back

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:15.305
to 1996, when there were
a lot of search companies,

00:03:15.305 --> 00:03:16.910
search was solved.

00:03:16.910 --> 00:03:21.140
We know all of these, or if
you were back there, back then

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:22.850
searching on the
internet you had

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:26.710
to go through all of them trying
to find whatever you wanted,

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:28.160
page number 10.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.170
It was hard, it was hard.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.500
They were all looking at the
same data, the public web.

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:35.420
They were all
collecting the web,

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.240
ranking the results in
the best way possible,

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:42.890
and serving it from the biggest
server that they could find.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:47.190
But then in 1998, Google shows
up, it looks at the same data,

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.310
it's the same web
pages, but instead

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:52.790
of looking only at the
content of each web page,

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.870
Google starts looking at the
relationships between them,

00:03:55.870 --> 00:03:58.040
the page rank algorithm.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.740
What I'm trying to
show here is that we

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:06.440
had a new idea, PageRank, just
with the same data collecting

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:12.560
the whole web and with a new
generation of technology.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.540
The technology that
Google used was

00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:16.640
pretty different to
what everyone else was

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:17.540
doing at the time.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:21.269
At the time, everyone was trying
the biggest server possible.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:24.380
Google had new technology ideas.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.200
And you could do the same.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.180
How do we empower your
ideas with more data?

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:33.920
How do we make--
instead of having

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.740
to collect all this data, how
can we collect it for you?

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:38.480
How can we share the
technology that we

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.560
have created with you to empower
whatever your next ideas are?

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.030
Because that's what
changes the world.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:50.940
Google on the technology
side, we've created--

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:55.220
at first we published
many of the papers that

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:59.150
revolutionized the big data
world with the internet

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:00.160
as we know today.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.290
It all started with the file
system, the Google file system

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:08.870
being able to store a
limited gigabytes of data.

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:13.670
And then MapReduce this idea
on, how do we move computing?

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:15.530
Instead of having
it in one computer,

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:19.780
orchestrating it on 1,000 of
them of cheaper computers.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:23.930
And so many other papers
that on the last few years

00:05:23.930 --> 00:05:27.860
we've been translating
that to a world where

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:31.040
instead of sharing paper,
we share the same technology

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:32.300
that we're using.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.470
All of these papers,
you can access them now

00:05:34.470 --> 00:05:36.080
off Google Cloud products.

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.950
You might have been
using some of them.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:42.540
The last one, this
one, it's public now.

00:05:42.540 --> 00:05:43.790
It's available now.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:45.350
You can use Spanner tool.

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:47.570
But I'm going to focus
on other products here

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:52.370
that allows us to
share and analyze data.

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:54.400
Now, when we think
about open data,

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.230
there's a lot of different
ways data can be shared.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:01.520
It's not only structured data,
CSVs, spreadsheets, et cetera.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:06.320
You can also have unstructured
data, text, annotations, DNA,

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:13.680
images of petabytes, of
satellites, pictures, of sky,

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:14.655
et cetera, et cetera.

00:06:14.655 --> 00:06:19.000
These All require
different tools.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:21.280
We don't want to be
another open data portal.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:23.410
Today there are
more than 500 data

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.950
portals all around the world.

00:06:26.950 --> 00:06:29.780
This is one that was being
counted by open knowledge,

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:30.800
yes, a civic data.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:33.032
But there are so many portals.

00:06:33.032 --> 00:06:34.490
We don't want to
replace them, they

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.190
are all doing an amazing job.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:37.540
We want to empower.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:40.390
We want to help
where we can help,

00:06:40.390 --> 00:06:43.080
where we can make
things different.

00:06:43.080 --> 00:06:45.640
To give you an
example, Stack Exchange

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:50.330
has their own portal with
the stack overflows data.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.900
So here you can
find questions, you

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:54.700
can find the queries
people can use.

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:57.910
You can run your own queries and
they let you run your queries

00:06:57.910 --> 00:06:59.350
on their server.

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:01.870
And it's really cool.

00:07:01.870 --> 00:07:05.080
It's a great place to
share, to learn, to analyze,

00:07:05.080 --> 00:07:05.890
to understand.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:07.600
It's fast, it's easy, it's cool.

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:11.170
But it has some limitations,
because at the end of the day,

00:07:11.170 --> 00:07:13.510
we're using their
server that has

00:07:13.510 --> 00:07:15.550
limitations so
they have to impose

00:07:15.550 --> 00:07:17.950
those limitations on us too.

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:21.820
The max number of rows you can
output, they don't have an API,

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:26.980
they don't allow robots,
queries sometimes time out,

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:28.960
and you cannot
mix your own data.

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.020
It's their server, you
don't want to upload data

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.450
to their server.

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:35.740
So in cases like this,
we can really help.

00:07:35.740 --> 00:07:39.310
We can go past
these limitations.

00:07:42.060 --> 00:07:44.340
When you're sharing
data, too many users

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:47.330
would be a problem, too many
questions, too many indexes,

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:51.280
the ability to run
any kind of question

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.735
is one of the
things we can offer.

00:07:53.735 --> 00:07:58.520
And especially the cost model,
because sharing one server,

00:07:58.520 --> 00:08:05.090
sharing your own servers can
become an expensive endeavor.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:06.710
With the tools I'm
going to show you,

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:09.710
you are able to share
data without bearing

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:12.230
the cost of the analysis.

00:08:12.230 --> 00:08:15.320
With BigQuery, with
Pub/Sub, the model

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:17.810
here is that the person
doing the analysis

00:08:17.810 --> 00:08:22.430
can pay for their own
analysis so it doesn't impact

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:24.410
your own ability to share.

00:08:27.210 --> 00:08:31.260
So we can create
a sharing platform

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:34.950
to help with bigger datasets,
with streaming datasets.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:37.400
We can bring
flexibility, like knowing

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:40.500
the access needed for
sharing, or if you

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:45.720
have a stream of data, help
with all of the security

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.310
and without needing
to configure anything.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:49.530
You can bring your own data.

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:52.020
People that want to
mix public datasets

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.870
with their own private
datasets, they can do everything

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:58.830
in the same platform, they
can scale in a serverless way,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:00.450
don't worry about
servers anymore.

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.810
You can distribute the cost
and we can also act as Google

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:05.610
as a data provider.

00:09:05.610 --> 00:09:11.850
With a public dataset program,
we want to help bring in data

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.270
and cleaning the data so
it can be analyzed in time.

00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:21.600
So this is the Google Cloud
Public Datasets Program,

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:27.300
what we have been working for
the last year, setting it up.

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:32.750
When I started working in this
role, I got a lot of datasets.

00:09:32.750 --> 00:09:34.140
And that big
dataset that I could

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:36.750
find, I'd put it into BigQuery,
I shared it with people,

00:09:36.750 --> 00:09:38.325
I shared an article.

00:09:38.325 --> 00:09:40.560
But that was a one-man job.

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.760
Right now we have a team,
we have a formal program

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:48.200
that's helping do
the same, but do it

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:51.780
in a productionized matter,
have these datasets updated,

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:54.510
have everything coming together.

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:56.930
Some benefits from the
Public Dataset Program

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:01.350
are that if you contact
us, we can give you

00:10:01.350 --> 00:10:03.640
free hosting and credits.

00:10:03.640 --> 00:10:08.560
If you want to share data,
we can help you with that.

00:10:08.560 --> 00:10:10.530
We can help you
setting up a pipeline

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:13.060
to update these
datasets every day,

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:15.980
every hour, when
and however we need.

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:18.480
And we can provide documentation
on cloud.google.com.

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.760
That's really good
for people that are

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:22.980
looking to work with this data.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:27.260
BigQuery has a free tier, 1
terabyte of monthly querying

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:29.016
quota for everyone.

00:10:29.016 --> 00:10:30.390
So if you share
data on BigQuery,

00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:34.950
anyone can come and analyze
these datasets at no cost

00:10:34.950 --> 00:10:36.190
up to a limit.

00:10:36.190 --> 00:10:39.896
But then if they want to do
much more, they're able to.

00:10:39.896 --> 00:10:42.490
We also added a Pub.

00:10:42.490 --> 00:10:46.300
We added a whole free tier
now for many other products

00:10:46.300 --> 00:10:50.830
and that was an announcement
that [INAUDIBLE] time.

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:53.200
With BigQuery and Pub/Sub,
you can distribute costs,

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:55.600
you can make things serverless,
you can make them open.

00:10:55.600 --> 00:11:00.010
I'm going to show you BigQuery
and Pub/Sub in a few slides.

00:11:00.010 --> 00:11:05.740
You just want to enable
people to combine datasets,

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:09.040
to bring new insights, to go
beyond analysis, to use it

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.800
with machine
learning, et cetera.

00:11:11.800 --> 00:11:14.830
So with this, I love
jumping into demo time

00:11:14.830 --> 00:11:17.860
because you might already
know this product,

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:22.840
but seeing them in use,
it's my favorite part.

00:11:22.840 --> 00:11:25.730
Who knows BigQuery?

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:28.040
A lot of hands, nice.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.640
The other half will
get to meet it, too.

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:34.100
BigQuery is our serverless
way of analyzing data.

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:36.800
It analyzes
terabytes in seconds,

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:40.100
you can use it just
by knowing SQL,

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:42.890
it scales from
bytes to petabytes.

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:44.780
There's nothing to
turn on, there's

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:48.920
no investment necessary, it's
just working all the time.

00:11:48.920 --> 00:11:50.930
It will work with
many other tools,

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:54.150
with Tableau, R,
Python, [INAUDIBLE],

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.900
it has a REST API agent,
JDBC connector, [INAUDIBLE]

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.350
connector.

00:11:58.350 --> 00:12:02.210
And the most interesting
part for me and for this talk

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:05.660
is being able to share
data instantly and giving

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:07.910
the free quota for everyone.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:10.850
And just to give you
some demos, these

00:12:10.850 --> 00:12:13.280
are some of the datasets
that we have loaded

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.770
on the Public Datasets
Program and that we

00:12:15.770 --> 00:12:17.870
are keeping updated.

00:12:17.870 --> 00:12:23.700
For example, let me jump
to the New York City taxis.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:26.060
Anyone knows how
many taxi rides there

00:12:26.060 --> 00:12:30.830
are in New York every
month or every year?

00:12:30.830 --> 00:12:31.442
Any guess?

00:12:31.442 --> 00:12:32.710
AUDIENCE: 6 million?

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:36.460
FELIPE HOFFA: 6 million taxi
rides in New York every month?

00:12:36.460 --> 00:12:37.170
More.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:41.190
Yes, way more.

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:41.890
Yes, way more.

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:46.500
I know the number
for every year,

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.530
but I need to divide it by 12.

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:54.190
So I have this all loaded in
BigQuery, I'm sharing it here.

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:59.580
New York public data, taxi
trips from 2009 to 2016.

00:12:59.580 --> 00:13:01.680
I know where to find
this, but normally people

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:05.490
don't know how to come here.

00:13:05.490 --> 00:13:11.990
So if I was looking for taxi
dataset on BigQuery, we are now

00:13:11.990 --> 00:13:14.660
offering a page
with documentation,

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:17.480
with the talks about
what's this dataset,

00:13:17.480 --> 00:13:20.022
how frequently do we update it.

00:13:20.022 --> 00:13:21.400
We have sample queries.

00:13:21.400 --> 00:13:24.740
For example, let's look
at the average speed

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:28.400
of yellow taxi trips in 2015.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:31.130
First, let me answer
the other question.

00:13:31.130 --> 00:13:36.490
This table for 2015
has many columns.

00:13:36.490 --> 00:13:39.140
And if we look at
the details, in 2015,

00:13:39.140 --> 00:13:44.560
New York City saw 146
million taxi rides.

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:48.415
That's a pretty big number.

00:13:48.415 --> 00:13:50.860
It's bigger than 8.

00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:53.270
If we multiply it by
12, it gets closer.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:55.144
Yeah, you were close.

00:13:55.144 --> 00:13:56.060
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:13:56.060 --> 00:13:58.080
FELIPE HOFFA: Yeah.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:14:00.360
So if we want to
write queries, we

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:03.610
can write simple queries like--

00:14:03.610 --> 00:14:08.520
let me query this table,
select, count, start.

00:14:08.520 --> 00:14:11.820
That's a simple
one, 145 million.

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:19.932
But then we're able to
share queries with BigQuery.

