run by Google Cloud or
other open source systems,

00:31:42.440 --> 00:31:44.420
like Flink or Spark.

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:51.030
Because Google Cloud Dataflow
has now the Beam API SDK,

00:31:51.030 --> 00:31:55.210
which is an Apache API, which
runs not only in Dataflow,

00:31:55.210 --> 00:31:57.890
but in other systems too.

00:31:57.890 --> 00:32:02.570
But there are some other talks
that will go deeper into that.

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:05.770
Who has worked with Pub/Sub?

00:32:05.770 --> 00:32:07.270
Yes.

00:32:07.270 --> 00:32:12.730
Pub/Sub is our solution
for distributing messages

00:32:12.730 --> 00:32:17.110
in real time, as
our queue system.

00:32:17.110 --> 00:32:21.360
With it, I'm able
to produce messages.

00:32:21.360 --> 00:32:24.040
Let's say this example
is from an IoT talk.

00:32:24.040 --> 00:32:29.290
If you have to distribute
messages from devices

00:32:29.290 --> 00:32:32.830
and send it to many
subscribers, with Pub/Sub

00:32:32.830 --> 00:32:35.210
you can do just that.

00:32:35.210 --> 00:32:38.120
Data producers can send
messages to Pub/Sub,

00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:41.110
Pub/Sub is always
ready to consume them,

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:44.170
there's nothing to configure.

00:32:44.170 --> 00:32:46.375
Then you can have
as many subscribers

00:32:46.375 --> 00:32:51.070
as you need getting each
of these messages in almost

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:58.420
real time, all managed
magically by Google.

00:32:58.420 --> 00:33:00.160
And the good news
for us, while I'm

00:33:00.160 --> 00:33:03.100
talking about Pub/Sub in this
talk, is because it also has--

00:33:05.650 --> 00:33:10.610
you can also share your
Pub/Sub datasets publicly.

00:33:10.610 --> 00:33:13.960
So if you need to publish
that in real time,

00:33:13.960 --> 00:33:16.810
you can send it here,
you can set it as public,

00:33:16.810 --> 00:33:20.050
and then anyone that needs to
ingest this out in real time,

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:21.280
can do it.

00:33:21.280 --> 00:33:24.260
We saw an example on the
keynote this morning.

00:33:24.260 --> 00:33:27.090
Let me show you
quickly that example.

00:33:30.202 --> 00:33:31.390
I will come back to this.

00:33:31.390 --> 00:33:33.280
I want to show you--

00:33:33.280 --> 00:33:37.940
this is from the New York City
Code Lab that we are running.

00:33:43.710 --> 00:33:46.530
So in this case, the problem
we wanted to solve here

00:33:46.530 --> 00:33:49.200
is, what happens
if New York wanted

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:52.600
to publish all of their
taxi rides in real time?

00:33:52.600 --> 00:33:57.300
They're not publishing them
right now for various reasons.

00:33:57.300 --> 00:34:01.830
But if they wanted, how
would they be able to do so?

00:34:01.830 --> 00:34:05.580
So we took all of the historical
New York City taxi data

00:34:05.580 --> 00:34:09.540
and we started publishing
on a public Pub/Sub feed.

00:34:09.540 --> 00:34:11.920
And then anyone with
Pub/Sub can subscribe.

00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:16.239
Here we created a
web page to do so.

00:34:16.239 --> 00:34:19.315
I can subscribe to any
of the public topics.

00:34:19.315 --> 00:34:24.904
I have this simulated feed
of taxi rides in real time

00:34:24.904 --> 00:34:28.659
and I can just start
fetching them as a consumer.

00:34:33.320 --> 00:34:35.060
You start just visualizing them.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:38.420
In this case, for this
example we are running--

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:45.481
this site is around the
4,000 messages per second.

00:34:45.481 --> 00:34:49.540
I'm right now receiving
2,000 data points per second

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:53.020
and just displaying them.

00:34:53.020 --> 00:34:56.350
The message here, the main
concept behind this demo

00:34:56.350 --> 00:35:01.880
is being able to share
messages in real time at scale

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:04.600
without managing anything.

00:35:04.600 --> 00:35:09.330
And you can go a step
further with Dataflow,

00:35:09.330 --> 00:35:11.600
that we were just talking about.

00:35:11.600 --> 00:35:15.410
With Dataflow you could take
those messages in real time

00:35:15.410 --> 00:35:19.820
and create a processing
system capable of transforming

00:35:19.820 --> 00:35:23.030
these elements, windowing
them, doing whatever operations

00:35:23.030 --> 00:35:24.200
you have to do.

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:28.190
And then publish either
to another Pub/Sub feed,

00:35:28.190 --> 00:35:32.560
publish to BigQuery,
publish to Files,

00:35:32.560 --> 00:35:35.750
just making your open
datasets, your real time

00:35:35.750 --> 00:35:39.380
datasets available for everyone.

00:35:39.380 --> 00:35:41.690
I don't have many public
datasets to share right now

00:35:41.690 --> 00:35:45.980
in real time in this program,
but I'm really looking forward

00:35:45.980 --> 00:35:49.660
to work with whoever
is ready and has

00:35:49.660 --> 00:35:53.340
been feeling this problem of
how to share data in real time.

00:35:53.340 --> 00:35:55.230
I'm starting with GitHub.

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:58.350
So for example, how do we get
from GitHub, every event that

00:35:58.350 --> 00:36:01.475
is happening in GitHub in real
time so you could subscribe?

00:36:01.475 --> 00:36:07.790
And the [INAUDIBLE] project
is helping us there.

00:36:07.790 --> 00:36:11.880
A couple more demos and
from the open source world

00:36:11.880 --> 00:36:13.920
because people always
ask me, show me

00:36:13.920 --> 00:36:16.905
that you can do this with
not only inside BigQuery,

00:36:16.905 --> 00:36:19.950
with other open source software.

00:36:19.950 --> 00:36:24.130
Who has worked with
Apache Zeppelin?

00:36:24.130 --> 00:36:25.260
OK, good.

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:28.650
So with Google, you're
able to run Apache Zeppelin

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:31.100
inside Dataproc.

00:36:31.100 --> 00:36:33.870
I launched my cluster
some minutes ago.

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:36.780
Let me see if I
can connect to it.

00:36:36.780 --> 00:36:41.000
So basically I
launched a cluster--

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:42.230
yeah, it's there.

00:36:42.230 --> 00:36:45.180
If you want to launch a
cluster, you say, hey, Dataproc,

00:36:45.180 --> 00:36:50.970
create a cluster with
this initialization action

00:36:50.970 --> 00:36:52.886
that will run Zeppelin.

00:36:52.886 --> 00:36:58.520
And once it's up in a couple of
minutes, you can connect to it.

00:36:58.520 --> 00:37:01.140
I just created an SSH tunnel.

00:37:01.140 --> 00:37:03.470
Here is my Zeppelin Notebook.

00:37:03.470 --> 00:37:07.390
And Zeppelin can now
connect to BigQuery.

00:37:07.390 --> 00:37:13.030
I have my interpreters
here, BigQuery here.

00:37:13.030 --> 00:37:16.350
I just need to tell it
this is my project ID

00:37:16.350 --> 00:37:21.655
and with that I can
start creating notebooks.

00:37:21.655 --> 00:37:23.980
I'm connected to
BigQuery, let me see.

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:31.380
Let's run a query that
we have already run.

00:37:31.380 --> 00:37:36.010
For example, what are the
most popular biking stations?

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:37.350
I can copy paste here.

00:37:44.330 --> 00:37:46.670
So when we document
this, we need to Escape.

00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:54.410
These are the most
popular bike stations.

00:37:54.410 --> 00:37:58.440
The most popular has
had 300,000 trips.

00:37:58.440 --> 00:38:00.890
And we can just
interact and bring it

00:38:00.890 --> 00:38:03.440
to different platforms.

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:06.766
Whatever is our favorite
platform to analyze data,

00:38:06.766 --> 00:38:11.760
we are able to
connect BigQuery to.

00:38:11.760 --> 00:38:14.060
The most popular.

00:38:14.060 --> 00:38:16.030
It doesn't look
too cool this way.

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:17.760
Let me see what looks cooler.

00:38:17.760 --> 00:38:22.390
But they all receive
a lot of trips.

00:38:22.390 --> 00:38:24.140
What I'm doing wrong?

00:38:24.140 --> 00:38:28.670
Oh, I'm looking at the
start station latitude.

00:38:28.670 --> 00:38:32.310
I want to get the
number of trips.

00:38:36.860 --> 00:38:39.120
Not the latitude.

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:44.330
So these are the most
popular biking stations.

00:38:44.330 --> 00:38:48.180
And the last visualization
demo I have is Data Studio.

00:38:48.180 --> 00:38:51.061
Who has worked with Data Studio?

00:38:51.061 --> 00:38:51.560
Good.

00:38:51.560 --> 00:38:52.730
We have some hands.

00:38:52.730 --> 00:38:54.590
We just made Data
Studio globally

00:38:54.590 --> 00:38:56.910
available for free everyone.

00:38:56.910 --> 00:38:58.880
So if you were
wondering when it's

00:38:58.880 --> 00:39:00.480
going to be available
in my country,

00:39:00.480 --> 00:39:02.550
it was available only in 22.

00:39:02.550 --> 00:39:04.630
Now it's available for everyone.

00:39:04.630 --> 00:39:08.360
You can come to Data
Studio, create a dashboard.

00:39:08.360 --> 00:39:13.760
If you don't know SQL, you
don't need to know SQL anymore.

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:16.850
And you can just, if you
have a BigQuery account,

00:39:16.850 --> 00:39:18.560
you can connect to BigQuery.

00:39:18.560 --> 00:39:21.590
Let me find public samples.

00:39:21.590 --> 00:39:22.760
How about we do--

00:39:26.952 --> 00:39:29.800
let's do names, just to show--

00:39:29.800 --> 00:39:35.240
or let's look at the most
popular names in the USA.

00:39:35.240 --> 00:39:40.420
The subject query table, I can
give it my building project.

00:39:40.420 --> 00:39:47.020
And I can connect to
any table on BigQuery,

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:49.420
Data Studio will
find the columns,

00:39:49.420 --> 00:39:52.035
and I can quickly come
and create, for example,

00:39:52.035 --> 00:39:59.830
a bar chart of names, what
are the most popular names?

00:39:59.830 --> 00:40:02.200
There have been more
males than females

00:40:02.200 --> 00:40:04.590
on these datasets so far.

00:40:04.590 --> 00:40:06.330
But instead of
looking at gender,

00:40:06.330 --> 00:40:09.030
let's look at the
most popular names.

00:40:11.550 --> 00:40:14.490
The most popular names are
James, John, Robert, Michael,

00:40:14.490 --> 00:40:17.321
William, Mary, David, Richard.

00:40:17.321 --> 00:40:17.820
OK.

00:40:21.240 --> 00:40:26.560
But I could also run a
filter and look it by state.

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:30.661
So this filter will do states.

00:40:34.070 --> 00:40:36.620
I can close this one.

00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:39.350
Now I have an
interactive dashboard.

00:40:39.350 --> 00:40:43.910
So for example, if I only
want to see California,

00:40:43.910 --> 00:40:48.470
I'm getting my results only
for California, or only

00:40:48.470 --> 00:40:52.150
for New York, or only for
Texas, et cetera, et cetera,

00:40:52.150 --> 00:40:52.940
et cetera.

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:57.120
But I have the freedom to
quickly create dashboards.

00:40:57.120 --> 00:41:02.990
I don't need to know any SQL, it
will just be able to create it.

00:41:02.990 --> 00:41:07.160
Also, it has this beautiful
share button, where I can also

00:41:07.160 --> 00:41:08.930
make this dashboard public.

00:41:08.930 --> 00:41:15.240
So anyone can come and run it
and start analyzing this data.

00:41:20.430 --> 00:41:23.520
Going back to the
presentation mode,

00:41:23.520 --> 00:41:27.270
the tools we were talking
about here is BigQuery,

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:32.310
you can analyze terabytes of
data in seconds just using SQL

00:41:32.310 --> 00:41:36.030
and you can share data, anyone
can access your dataset.

00:41:36.030 --> 00:41:37.950
With Pub/Sub you
can share streams.

00:41:37.950 --> 00:41:40.350
With Cloud Storage, that
I didn't review much,

00:41:40.350 --> 00:41:44.590
but with Cloud Storage
you can share files.

00:41:44.590 --> 00:41:47.730
And you have plenty of tools,
Dataflow, Dataproc, Datalab,

00:41:47.730 --> 00:41:50.992
Data Studio, CloudML,
Spreadsheets.

00:41:50.992 --> 00:41:52.950
These are tools that
Google provides to analyze

00:41:52.950 --> 00:41:54.560
and visualize this data.

00:41:54.560 --> 00:41:57.300
And you can also use a
lot of third party tools

00:41:57.300 --> 00:41:58.860
that we'll happily connect.

00:41:58.860 --> 00:42:01.860
[INAUDIBLE], she's my favorite
open source dashboard,

00:42:01.860 --> 00:42:04.710
Exploratorium, there are
so many other third party

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:07.680
tools that connect to this.

00:42:07.680 --> 00:42:11.940
The whole point is, how
do we empower new ideas?

00:42:11.940 --> 00:42:14.660
Not only in the ability
to analyze data, also

00:42:14.660 --> 00:42:16.650
in the machine learning world.

00:42:16.650 --> 00:42:20.610
Having this data available
which will revolutionize things.

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:23.460
We keep adding datasets
to the program.

00:42:23.460 --> 00:42:24.960
Anyone can share
their own datasets.

00:42:24.960 --> 00:42:28.560
But these are the ones that
we are grooming, and taking

00:42:28.560 --> 00:42:31.290
care of, and keeping
updated, and we

00:42:31.290 --> 00:42:36.046
keep adding more datasets

00:42:36.046 --> 00:42:39.582
If you want our help
or you're thinking

00:42:39.582 --> 00:42:42.040
that there is a very interesting
dataset that will help you

00:42:42.040 --> 00:42:44.290
to have here, please tell us.

00:42:44.290 --> 00:42:46.480
Yes, I'll let you take
a picture of the email.

00:42:49.380 --> 00:42:54.240
We really want to bring as much
useful data as we can here.

00:43:01.720 --> 00:43:04.650
You can share that
picture with me later.

00:43:04.650 --> 00:43:06.040
Just to run a
quick query here, I

00:43:06.040 --> 00:43:08.920
was looking at the number of
rows we're sharing right now,

00:43:08.920 --> 00:43:11.530
a count start off
all the datasets

00:43:11.530 --> 00:43:15.190
I listed previously,
it's 26 million rows.

00:43:15.190 --> 00:43:16.950
There's way more
that we can share

00:43:16.950 --> 00:43:18.940
and there's way
more that is being

00:43:18.940 --> 00:43:21.400
shared outside of the program.

00:43:21.400 --> 00:43:24.800
If you want to share a dataset
that you have on BigQuery,

00:43:24.800 --> 00:43:28.890
you can go and look at
the dataset, press share.

00:43:28.890 --> 00:43:31.390
As you can share it with
anyone in particular,

00:43:31.390 --> 00:43:34.690
you can share it with
all authenticated users,

00:43:34.690 --> 00:43:37.480
and that basically
makes it public.

00:43:37.480 --> 00:43:40.690
Now, no one will find it if you
don't tell them where it is.

00:43:40.690 --> 00:43:43.930
So we also want to have a
place where we can tell people,

00:43:43.930 --> 00:43:47.240
hey, my dataset is
here, it's documented,

00:43:47.240 --> 00:43:50.060
et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:43:50.060 --> 00:43:54.150
So the world is using open data
in many, many, many fields.

00:43:54.150 --> 00:43:58.750
There is more than 500 data
portals, as I was telling you.

00:43:58.750 --> 00:44:00.580
Especially if you are
one of these portals,

00:44:00.580 --> 00:44:06.100
we would love to help to make
your datasets more available.

00:44:06.100 --> 00:44:10.690
Let people query
them, combine them.

00:44:10.690 --> 00:44:14.370
We have the perfect
platform for that.

00:44:14.370 --> 00:44:16.630
In closing, the two
things to remember

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:21.140
is that we have awesome tools
to share and analyze data

00:44:21.140 --> 00:44:24.600
and the Public Datasets
Program is here to support you.

